---
<OpenerLayout
  title="Learn to Play"
  description="Start here: your first roll, your first Push, and your first Titan."
  section="learn"
  runhead={['Part I · Learn to Play', 'Before your first Expedition']}
  eyebrow="Part I"
  lede="Your first roll, your first Push, your first Titan."
  plate={{
    number: 'Plate II',
    caption: 'Cadets at drill on the practice frames.',
    label: 'Cadets flying on ODM Gear between wooden practice towers while an instructor watches from the ground',
    art: 'learn-opener',
    image: plate,
    maxWidth: 560,
  }}
  stamp="Read first"
>
  <FieldQuote cite="Survey Corps manual, issued to every recruit">
    You will be taught three things: how to fly, how to cut, and how to come back. Only the third is ever in doubt.
  </FieldQuote>

  <p><b>Wings of Freedom</b> is a tabletop roleplaying game about the Survey Corps. One player is the GM and plays the world; everyone else plays a soldier. Together you are a <GlossaryTerm term="Squad" /> of six — player characters, and <GlossaryTerm term="Squadmate">Squadmates</GlossaryTerm> to make up the number.</p>

  <p>A session is talk, then dice. Fights are short and lethal. <b>Only one cut kills a Titan</b> — the one at the back of its neck, reached only from behind. So somebody has to be the one it is looking at.</p>

  <p><b>What you need.</b> A GM, a few players, and ordinary six-sided dice in three colours. A sheet and a pencil each. Every table in the game is on this site, and the <a href="/reference/dice-tray/">Dice Tray</a> rolls for you if your dice are in another room.</p>

  <p class="lbl learn-path-label">The path</p>
  <ul class="contents">
    {path.map((item) => (
      <li>
        <a href={item.href}>
          <span class="cnum" aria-hidden="true"><Icon name="ph:arrow-down" class="arrow" /></span>
          <span class="ctitle">{item.title}</span>
          <span class="csum">{item.summary}</span>
          <span class="cmeta lbl">{item.meta}</span>
        </a>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  <section slot="after" class="sheet sheet--light rules" aria-labelledby="your-first-roll">
    <div class="runhead lbl"><span>Part I · Learn to Play</span><span>A walkthrough in five steps</span></div>
    <div class="rules-body">

      <SectionHead id="your-first-roll" eyebrow="Step 1">Your First Roll</SectionHead>

      <p>Build a pool of six-sided dice, roll it, count the sixes. <b>Every 6 is a success</b>, whatever die shows it. Dice are never added up.</p>

      <p>Your pool is your attribute, plus one <GlossaryTerm term="Talent" /> that names what you are doing, plus any <GlossaryTerm term="Bonus Dice" />, plus <GlossaryTerm term="Gear Dice" /> from one item, plus a <GlossaryTerm term="Stress Dice">Stress Die</GlossaryTerm> per point of <GlossaryTerm term="Stress" />, less penalties. <b>Most rolls need 1 success.</b> The few that need more take their number from something real — how deep a Titan's <GlossaryTerm term="Nape" /> lies.</p>

      <DiceKey />

      <MarginNote>The seven steps of a pool, and the Circumstances the GM names first: <a href="/guide/rules-of-play/#building-the-pool">The Rules of Play</a>.</MarginNote>

      <FieldReport id="example-toma-repair" title="Toma mends a harness">

        <p>Field Repair names Wits, takes a tool kit's dice, and needs 1 success. Toma has <b>Wits 4</b>, a <b>tool kit rated 2</b>, no Stress, and no Talent for it.</p>

        <PoolRows rows={[{ label: 'The roll', pool: 'base:4,6,2,3|gear:5,1', successes: 1 }]} />

        <p>One 6 out of six dice. The buckle holds. <b>That is a whole roll.</b></p>

        <TryRollButton pool={{ base: 4, gear: 2 }} label="Try Toma's roll" />

      </FieldReport>

      <SectionHead id="pushing-your-luck" eyebrow="Step 2">Pushing Your Luck</SectionHead>

      <p>A <GlossaryTerm term="Push" /> trades Stress, and sometimes gear, for a second chance — after a failure, or after a success you wanted more from.</p>

      <PushSteps fig="Fig. 1" />

      <p><b>You cannot Push</b> when a Stress Die showed a 1, when the rule forbids it, or when you have Pushed once already. A comrade can <GlossaryTerm term="Cover" /> your Push instead, taking the Stress in your place.</p>

      <FieldReport id="example-toma-push" title="Toma Pushes, and pays for it">

        <p>Same repair, now at Stress 1.</p>

        <PoolRows rows={[
          { label: 'First roll', pool: 'base:5,3,2,4|gear:1,4|stress:2', successes: 0 },
          { label: 'After the Push', pool: 'base:6,2,5,3|gear:1l,3|stress:5,3n', successes: 1 },
        ]} />

        <p>Nothing, and no Stress Die on 1 — so he Pushes: 1 Stress, a new Stress Die, and everything but the 6s rolled again. <b>His Gear Die showing 1 is locked; the other rolls again.</b></p>

        <p>One 6. The harness is mended, his Stress is 2 — and because he Pushed, the locked Gear Die showing 1 wears his tool kit down a point. One point, however many had shown 1.</p>

        <TryRollButton pool={{ base: 4, gear: 2, stress: 1 }} label="Try Toma's Push" />

      </FieldReport>

      <MarginNote stamp="Read twice">Stress carries between fights, Expeditions, and sessions, and drops only through the triggers on the Stress table: <a href="/guide/rules-of-play/#stress">The Rules of Play</a>.</MarginNote>

      <p><b>What Stress buys and costs.</b> Each point is another die in every pool — and another chance of a 1. A Stress Die showing 1 sets off a <GlossaryTerm term="Stress Response" />: your nerve gives out for a moment. One per roll, however many 1s fall.</p>

      <InkVignette caption="A soldier counts her red dice into her palm while the gate crew works the winches." label="A soldier counting dice in her hand before a gate opens" art="learn-stress" />

      <SectionHead id="your-soldier" eyebrow="Step 3">Your Soldier in One Minute</SectionHead>

      <IconKey title="The six attributes" items={[
        { icon: 'attr-strength', label: 'Strength' },
        { icon: 'attr-agility', label: 'Agility' },
        { icon: 'attr-wits', label: 'Wits' },
        { icon: 'attr-perception', label: 'Perception' },
        { icon: 'attr-instinct', label: 'Instinct' },
        { icon: 'attr-empathy', label: 'Empathy' },
      ]} />

      <Subsection title="What is on the sheet" id="the-sheet">

        <ul>
          <li><b>Attributes,</b> rated 2 to 5 and never raised after creation. Only your Specialty's key attribute can reach 6.</li>
          <li><b><GlossaryTerm term="Health" /> and <GlossaryTerm term="Resolve" />,</b> worked out from those attributes. Health is what you lose before you are <GlossaryTerm term="Down" />; Resolve counts against a Stress Response or a <GlossaryTerm term="Fear Roll" />.</li>
          <li><b>A <GlossaryTerm term="Specialty" /></b> — Slayer, Medic, Engineer, Flier, and five more. It sets your key attribute and gives you a Talent. <b>It grants no permission:</b> every soldier may attempt every action, and one without the Talent still rolls their full attribute.</li>
          <li><b>Talents,</b> five levels at creation. A dice Talent adds base dice to the actions it names; a rule Talent bends one rule whenever its trigger fires.</li>
          <li><b>Gear,</b> issued: <GlossaryTerm term="ODM Gear" />, a <GlossaryTerm term="Blade Set" />, gas, a horse. An item's current rating is its Gear Dice, and it wears down with hard use.</li>
          <li><b>An Origin, a <GlossaryTerm term="Haven" />, and a <GlossaryTerm term="Drive" />:</b> where you are from, what you go back to, and what keeps you going out.</li>
        </ul>

      </Subsection>

      <MarginNote>Roll a <GlossaryTerm term="Lifepath" /> or build step by step: <a href="/guide/making-your-soldier/">Making Your Soldier</a>. Every Specialty and Talent: <a href="/compendium/specialties/">Compendium</a>.</MarginNote>

      <p>The fastest way in is the <b>Lifepath</b>: roll where your soldier grew up, why they enlisted, and what the Training Corps made of them, and the sheet fills itself in.</p>

      <SectionHead id="your-first-titan" eyebrow="Step 4">Your First Titan</SectionHead>

      <FieldQuote cite="A Squad Leader, to a new recruit">While it is watching you, you are not cutting. That is the trade. Make it worth something.</FieldQuote>

      <p>A <GlossaryTerm term="Titan Engagement" /> runs in rounds. Each round you take one turn: one <b>move</b> and one <b>action</b>. Your move changes where you stand relative to one Titan by one step.</p>

      <PositionsMap fig="Fig. 2" rating="urban" />

      <Subsection title="Attention" id="attention">
        <p>A Titan's <GlossaryTerm term="Attention" /> is held by one soldier, a decoy, or nothing — and everyone can see who. When its card comes up it reads its <GlossaryTerm term="Attention Ladder" /> and turns to whoever meets the highest rung anyone meets. <b>The soldier holding its Attention cannot strike its Nape.</b> That one line is the whole of squad tactics: somebody stands where it will look, somebody else goes round the back.</p>
      </Subsection>

      <Subsection title="The Nape strike" id="the-nape-strike">
        <p>A Strength roll on your Blade Set, from its <GlossaryTerm term="Blind Spot" />, not holding its Attention and not <GlossaryTerm term="Grabbed" />. Reach its <GlossaryTerm term="Nape Depth" /> in successes and it dies. <b>Fall short and each success becomes an <GlossaryTerm term="Opening" /></b> — which a comrade spends as a Bonus Die. Never you: your own Openings wait on its body for somebody else.</p>
        <p><b>Blind Spot is not on the Titan.</b> It is a roof, a tree, a beam behind it: you are out of its sight with its Nape within reach, and you are not touching it. <b><GlossaryTerm term="On Body" /> is the one that means on it.</b> Get this the wrong way round and half the rules of a fight stop making sense.</p>
        <p>The strike does not move you either. You are still at Blind Spot afterwards, and since the strike needs a soldier the Titan is not looking at, a striker who pulls its eye onto themselves cannot strike again until somebody else takes it.</p>
      </Subsection>

      <Subsection title="It gets worse the longer you take" id="frenzy">
        <p>Every Titan carries a <GlossaryTerm term="Frenzy" />: 0 when the fight starts, and 1 more at the end of every round, up to 3. That number is added to its behavior roll, and every Titan's table runs from frightening you to killing you. <b>Round one it postures. By round three it is trying to kill somebody.</b></p>
        <p>And it does not waste a turn. If the soldier it is fixed on is standing somewhere it cannot reach, it turns on whoever it can. Hanging behind a Titan keeps <em>you</em> safe. It does not keep your squad safe.</p>
      </Subsection>

      <Subsection title="When it swings at you" id="when-it-swings">
        <p><GlossaryTerm term="Titan Dice" /> are rolled in the open and succeed on a <b>5 or 6</b>. Those successes are its <GlossaryTerm term="Severity" />, announced to every target. Answer with a dodge — Agility on your ODM Gear — and each success cancels one. What is left is the <GlossaryTerm term="Net Successes" />, and on 1 or more it lands. <b>A dodge spends a whole turn</b>, so a soldier under a Titan's eye runs out of time.</p>
      </Subsection>

      <MarginNote>Titans never Push and never react. Everything a Titan does is one roll, made where you can see it, and it stands.</MarginNote>

      <FieldReport id="example-first-fight" title="One fight, from the first move" eyebrow="Example of play">

        <p>A Medium Titan in a ruined street: <b>Urban</b> ground, <b>3 Anchors</b>, everyone at Distant, <GlossaryTerm term="Nape Depth" /> 4. Every move on the lines is a <b>Flight</b> — roll Fly, take the step whatever the dice give, and bank 1 <b>Momentum</b> per success to buy extra steps later. Roll nothing and you come in loud.</p>

        <p><b>Round 1.</b> Anselm walks from Distant to In Reach. The Titan's card comes next: nobody is hooked into its body, so the nearest person In Reach takes its eye — and only Anselm is there. Its behavior is the Grab.</p>

        <PoolRows rows={[
          { label: "The Titan's attack", pool: 'titan:5,2,6,3,6,1,4,2,3', successes: 3 },
          { label: "Anselm's dodge", pool: 'base:6,3,2|gear:6,4|stress:5', successes: 2 },
        ]} />

        <p>Severity 3 against two dodge successes. <b>One left over is enough.</b> The hand closes on him.</p>

        <TryRollButton pool={{ base: 3, gear: 2, stress: 1 }} label="Try Anselm's dodge" />

        <p>Nadja's card is last. Her Flight rolls 2 successes, so she banks 2 Momentum and steps to In Reach — then spends 1 to carry the same move on to <b>Blind Spot</b>, behind it, with 1 still in hand.</p>

      </FieldReport>

      <p><b>What a Grab means.</b> Anselm takes a crushing <GlossaryTerm term="Critical Injury" /> that cannot kill him, holds On Body, and cannot <GlossaryTerm term="Help" />, Cover, or dodge. His only action is Break Free. Everyone who saw it makes a Fear Roll. Then a clock starts, counted in his own turns.</p>

      <GrabCountdown fig="Fig. 3" />

      <MarginNote>Prying a comrade loose, cutting the arm that holds them, and Clear the Hand: <a href="/guide/fighting-titans/#the-grab">Fighting Titans</a>.</MarginNote>

      <FieldReport id="example-first-fight-end" title="Two rounds to get him out" eyebrow="Example of play">

        <p>His dodge spent a whole turn, so his next turn arrives already spent — and it is the first the Grab counts. Lifted at the end of it, devoured at the end of the next. <b>The Squad has two rounds.</b></p>

        <p><b>Round 2.</b> While the Titan holds a living soldier its card resolves <em>nothing</em>: no ladder, no behavior, no attack. That silence is the Squad's whole chance.</p>

        <p>Nadja can strike, because Anselm holds its Attention: 5 base, 2 Gear, 1 Stress against Nape Depth 4.</p>

        <PoolRows rows={[{ label: "Nadja's strike", pool: 'base:6,3,5,2,6|gear:6,4|stress:1x', successes: 3 }]} />

        <p>Three — one short, and her Stress Die came up 1, so she cannot Push. Her three successes become <b>3 Openings she can never spend herself</b>. Toma flies in beside her.</p>

        <p><b>Round 3.</b> Toma spends all three Openings as Bonus Dice: 6 base, 1 Gear, 2 Stress.</p>

        <PoolRows rows={[{ label: "Toma's cut", pool: 'base:6,2,6,4,3,6|gear:6|stress:4,2', successes: 4 }]} />

        <p>Four successes — the Nape Depth exactly. The Titan dies, the hand opens, and everyone holding a Position sheds Stress for the kill.</p>

        <TryRollButton pool={{ base: 6, gear: 1, stress: 2 }} label="Try Toma's cut" />

        <p><b>A dying Titan is not safe ground:</b> steam scalds everyone at its Nape, and the body comes down on whoever is still on it. <a href="/guide/fighting-titans/#killing-it">Fighting Titans</a> has that order — and everything this fight left out.</p>

      </FieldReport>

      <SectionHead id="where-to-go-next" eyebrow="Step 5">Where to Go Next</SectionHead>

      <p>That is the game: a pool of dice, a choice about Stress, and a Titan that cannot see behind itself. The rest is detail, and it is all here.</p>

      <ul class="contents">
        {next.map((item) => (
          <li>
            <a href={item.href}>
              <span class="cnum" aria-hidden="true"><Icon name={item.icon} class="arrow" /></span>
              <span class="ctitle">{item.title}</span>
              <span class="csum">{item.summary}</span>
              <span class="cmeta lbl">Open</span>
            </a>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>

    </div>
  </section>
</OpenerLayout>

<style>
  .learn-path-label {
    margin-top: clamp(26px, 3.5vw, 40px);
    margin-bottom: 10px;
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }
</style>
